<Project>
  <!--
    Define $(SolutionDir) for direct `dotnet build` invocations.

    Many csprojs in this repo reference sibling projects via
    `<ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)\..." />`. When MSBuild is
    driven from a .sln (Rider, VS, `dotnet build Src/WPR.sln`), SolutionDir
    is set automatically and those references resolve to Src/. When
    MSBuild is invoked on a single csproj (`dotnet build Src/Core/Foo/Foo.csproj`),
    SolutionDir is *Undefined* — the references silently fail to resolve
    and the consuming compile errors out with "namespace not found"
    cascades that look like a transitive-restore bug.

    Setting it here, scoped to the Src/ tree and only when undefined,
    keeps the .sln path unchanged while letting `dotnet build <csproj>`
    work as expected. Trailing slash matches the VS/Rider convention so
    `$(SolutionDir)\ThirdParty\...` joins correctly either way.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(SolutionDir)' == '' or '$(SolutionDir)' == '*Undefined*' ">
    <SolutionDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)</SolutionDir>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    THE version number for the product. Set it here and nowhere else.

    Before 2026-08-30 it was typed by hand in five places and had already drifted: AboutPage said
    0.0.20 while the main window title still said 0.0.18, and the android manifest pinned 0.0.18
    in a way that silently overrode what the release workflow passed in (see
    Platforms/WPR.Platform.Android/Properties/AndroidManifest.xml).

    Consumers:
      * WPR.Platform.Windows  — $(Version) / $(InformationalVersion); the UI reads the latter back
        at runtime via AppVersion.Display, so the window title and About page cannot drift again.
      * WPR.Platform.Android  — $(ApplicationDisplayVersion), i.e. the APK versionName.
      * .github/workflows/release.yml — overrides both with -p:Version / -p:ApplicationDisplayVersion
        from the dispatch input, so a release does not require editing this file. Bump it here
        anyway so local/dev builds report the version you are actually working towards.

    Deliberately NOT setting <Version> repo-wide: several projects pin a version that is part of a
    WP7 assembly identity (WPR.Framework.Devices.Location is 2.0.5.0 to match System.Device), and
    those must keep their own value. Project-level properties win over this file, but the heads are
    the only things that should carry the product version, so they opt in explicitly.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- 0.1.0 -> 0.1.01 on 2026-08-31, -> 0.1.02 on 2026-09-03. Note the two-digit patch: it satisfies release.yml's
         ^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$ check and reaches Android's versionName verbatim, but the Windows
         head feeds $(WprVersion) into <Version>, and AssemblyVersion/FileVersion parse "01" as 1 —
         so desktop assembly metadata reads 0.1.2 while the displayed product version reads 0.1.02.
         Use a single-digit patch if you want those to agree. -->
    <WprVersion>0.1.02</WprVersion>
    <!-- There is deliberately no version SUFFIX. $(WprVersionSuffix) was "alpha" and the Windows
         head appended it to InformationalVersion, so the window title and About page read
         "0.1.01-alpha". Dropped for 0.1.02: the displayed version is now exactly $(WprVersion).
         If a pre-release build is ever wanted again, prefer release.yml's `prerelease` input,
         which flags the GitHub release without baking a suffix into shipped assembly metadata. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Single EF Core version for the whole repo.

    Until 2026-08-30 this was a per-project, TFM-conditional property: 5.0.17 on
    net8.0-windows10.0.17763.0 and 6.0.8 everywhere else, duplicated across
    WPR.Loader / WPR.Runtime / Microsoft.Xna.Framework.GamerServices, with
    WPR.Platform.Windows hardcoding 5.0.17 a fourth time. That split silently pinned the
    desktop app to a .NET 5-era dependency graph and was the root of most of the security
    advisories cleared on 2026-08-29/30.

    Unifying on 8.0.x is safe here because nothing in this repo runs EF migrations: both
    databases ship as pre-built .db files (Core/WPR.Database) that are copied into place on
    first run, and the 2022 Migrations/ folders are never executed. The EF surface actually
    used is AsNoTracking / SaveChanges(Async) / AddRange / RemoveRange / Find, all of which
    behave identically from EF 5 through 8. EF 8 also wants SQLitePCLRaw.core >= 2.1.6,
    which the 2.1.13 pin in WPR.Loader / GamerServices already satisfies.

    Every consuming TFM (net8.0, net8.0-windows10.0.17763.0, net8.0-android) is net8.0+, so
    EF 8's minimum target is met on all of them.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <EFVersion>8.0.30</EFVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('$(TargetFramework)').Contains('android'))">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);__ANDROID__;__MOBILE__</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
